  {
    "episode_id": 95663,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95663.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 175,
    "turn_text": "So I can tell you from the day I just decided to run for Congress, which by the way, the morning after the 2016 election, was at the breakfast table with my two teenage daughters at that time, and I promised them I would do something. Their reaction to that election really left an indelible mark on me, and I decided I was gonna do something that became a run for Congress. And I learned so quickly just how disgusting the pursuit of campaign cash is in our country. And my colleagues right now spend 10,000 hours per week raising money collectively in the House and Senate. dialing for dollars, going to events, evenings, hard to find colleagues to have dinner with in DC when we're in session because most have to go to pack events where they're handed checks and little white envelopes and they go event to event, they go to breakfast, they go to lunch. In fact, I introduced a bill just a couple weeks ago to ban the raising of money while by members of congress while we are in session in washington between breakfast and dinner you want to do it before breakfast after dinner that's fine but not on the taxpayers dying because it's a massive problem and i call it the influence of affluence",
