  {
    "episode_id": 95663,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95663.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 230,
    "turn_text": "So in a campaign, a candidate can solicit you for a contribution. And in a congressional campaign, there's a cap that's $2,900. before the primary, and it's $2,900 after the primary. So $5,800 is the maximum amount an individual can contribute to a candidate during what we call a cycle, two years in between. So that's how the caps are done for individuals, for dark money groups, for I.E.' 's independent expenditure committees. Those are just groups that are set up. They cannot coordinate with campaigns. They can't work with a candidate to say, you do this, we'll do that. Here's what we're going to run. There's supposed to be a wall between the two, although I think collusion happens all the time. And what these dark money groups do is raise money from gazillionaires all around the country and then determine what races they want to influence. And then they simply Create ads, oftentimes attacking the opponent of the person they're supporting, often using misinformation and disinformation to make their point. And again, oftentimes it appears that it might be the candidate doing the ad, but it's not. And that's why the transparency issue is such a big deal, is that we don't even know when we're seeing these ads who's actually running them. And it allows candidates to wipe their hands and say, hey, You know, I promised to run a nice thoughtful campaign. That wasn't me that ran that nasty app. You know, that was somebody else. Well, in the meantime, you know, they're laughing all the way to the polling station. So that's how that game works. And that's what I ease are. We call my independent expenditure groups and they're separate from campaigns. Then you also have the national parties. The DCCC, the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, the RNCC, the Republican National Campaign Committee, the Democratic Senate Campaign Committee, the Republican Senate Campaign Committee, the Democratic governors, the Republican governors, every one of these groups raises tens of millions of dollars every cycle. And you just don't know who's behind it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
